{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And he turned and went out from Pharaoh</b> — Because they were greatly frightened by the hail, Moses thought that now too they would be frightened that they would die of hunger if the rest of the remnant left to them were destroyed, and he went out without his permission, before they answered him yes or no, so that they would take counsel about the matter. He thought true thoughts, for so they did and said to Pharaoh, <b>Do you not yet know that Egypt is destroyed?</b> And in the words of our Sages (Exodus Rabbah 13:4): he saw them turning to one another, believing his words, so he went out from there in order that they might take counsel to do repentance. And the correct view in my eyes is that this is what he would do every time he came to Pharaoh, to his palace — he would warn him and go out from his presence. But Scripture needed to mention it here on account of \"and Moses was brought back\" (Exodus 10:8)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:37.705955597Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}